00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:04.490 align:start position:0%
 
hello  my name is Caroline I'm a sports  reporter and for the past three years  I've lived in Tokyo  I'm fascinated by Japan a country of  contradictions in vibrant sometimes  through subcultures things are often  completely different than in the West  normally I work behind the cameras for  instance in this piece for DW kick-off  about a futuristic soccer pitch  we're here on the roof of the train  station the location is awesome Shibuya  is full of shops in dat young people  most people don't know you can play  football up here  I also reported for kickoff about fans  watching the 2014 World Cup final at  Tsukiji the world's biggest fish market  Japanese prefer to watch matches at home  so during a big game that's hardly  anyone on the streets it was an  unbelievable experience from East German  to watch my national team win the World  Cup  Monday morning between tuna and mackerel  recently I returned to Germany the  German capital to be precise Berlin it  was hard to get readjusted to my former  hometown I needed to go somewhere  familiar Olympic Stadium I quickly  realized the atmosphere hadn't changed  and the same collection of characters is  still hanging out there  Berlin's Herto made headlines in the far  east when they signed Genki Haraguchi  this season he's the youngest of 13  Japanese players in the Bundesliga and  then it happened I was there to witness  the biggest moment of Kinky's young  Bundesliga career it's in the net  against Shaka  what a moment I imagine it must be  difficult to have sporting success in a  foreign and unfamiliar country I want to  know how the young Japanese forward is  getting on in Germany so I grabbed him  one day after practice on planet pattern  my name is Genki Haraguchi can i play  for her to Berlin I've been here since  July 2014 almost a year his first year  at Herto hasn't been easy his first  coach got fired and now the team is  battling relegation under the new man in  charge paladar died how is it to face  sporting challenges while having to get  used to new city it's nice living in  Berlin there's nothing that puts me off  Germans and Japanese are pretty similar  everything is orderly people are  punctual and stick to agreements nothing  runs according to people's wind power  gucci is the big name in japan he began  his career with the Arava red diamonds a  team that said german coach is like  Keita booth white or like here from the  back Foca finca they usually topped the  table and their fans are passionate her  Iguchi had a heavy heart when he left  and his farewell in Saitama stadium left  me misty-eyed  we've wept together in sadness and in  joy you stood behind me no matter what  so did my teammates my coaches the  officials my friends and family and of  course my girlfriend  of course the j-league is great but in  Japan they place more emphasis on  organized systematic team play here the  players are a lot freer and the  one-on-ones are a lot harder 3303 not  the Kansai that's why I need to improve  in the beginning I realized I wasn't  strong enough so I started doing  additional workouts I think that  nowadays I'm a lot stronger than I used  to be  come here you don't have the strict  hierarchical differences between young  and old that you have in Japan it's  normal for players to joke around and  embrace one another everyone's equal  within the team personally I think  that's great talking to her Iguchi is  like being back in Japan for a bit we  locate a cherry tree and immerse  ourselves in the world of calligraphy by  the way Genki means both how are you and  energy vitality lifeforce courage path  and so on you get the picture  this came out okay  as for me I have learned how much  football makes me feel at home whether  in Berlin or in Tokyo I'm sure that  thanks to young football ambassadors  like Yankee Japan will be paying more  attention to all the stars in the  Bundesliga galaxy  go say ciao arigato gozaimasu they were  matinee  you

